welcome to angular firebase today I'm  going to show you how to create separate  back-end environments for development  and production with angular for  maintaining separate environments allows  you to build and test new features  without screwing up data from your live  production app step one create two fire  based projects before jumping into the  code you'll need two separate projects  on fire firebase one for development and  one for production step 2 create the  environment files in this case I have  cloned the fire starter app from github  and the first step is to create the  environment file files manually if you  create a new app with the angular CLI  then these files are going to be here by  default and then also make sure to add  these files to your git ignore because  they hold sensitive credentials and you  don't want that ending up in a public  repo step 3  paste in the firebase configuration into  each of the environment files step 4  bootstrap the environment in the app  module now we can import the environment  variables anywhere in our app in this  case we want to bootstrap the firebase  configuration directly in the app module  the code you're looking at right now is  what is specifically needed to wire up  angular firebase with these separate  environments in your app module  step 5 serve or compile the app with  that done it's possible to serve the app  locally using either environment all we  have to do is add the prod to ng serve  and that'll serve the app in production  thanks for watching I'll see you on the  other side  [Music]  you
 

